	abstract = {The development of text-to-SQL systems has been constrained by the scarcity of high-quality training data. Due to the high cost and limited scalability of manual annotation, researchers have increasingly turned to large language models (LLMs) to generate synthetic data. However, existing synthetic data generation methods often produce questions that are not representative of real-world scenarios, and there is frequently a lack of strict semantic alignment between the natural language queries (NLQs) and their corresponding SQL statements. This limits the effectiveness of model training and hampers performance in practical deployments. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a novel NLQ-SQL data synthesis framework embedded with an error feedback mechanism. The method introduces a structured feedback loop that automatically detects, categorizes, and feeds synthesis errors back to the LLM, enabling iterative optimization of the model and continuous improvement in data quality. This closed-loop mechanism enhances the realism and semantic alignment of the generated SQL queries, making them more reflective of actual business scenarios. Experimental results based on standard text-to-SQL benchmarks demonstrate that the proposed approach significantly improves the generalization capability of text-to-SQL models © 2025 IEEE.},
