	abstract = {Text-to-SQL aims to convert natural language questions into executable SQL queries for retrieving information from databases. Despite significant advancements, a key limitation in current research is the lack of focus on geospatial databases. This paper addresses that gap by exploring LLM-based Text-to-SQL for geospatial information retrieval. We first construct a new benchmark using a PostGIS spatial database, specifically designed for Text-to-SQL tasks in the geospatial domain. Specifically, we use LLMs to generate additional user-SQL query pairs, enhancing the benchmark's size and diversity. We also provide a statistical analysis of its characteristics, including query length, SQL complexity, and difficulty levels. Afterwards, we implement a Text-to-SQL pipeline leveraging state-of-theart (SOTA) large language models, including GPT-4, Llama, and Google's Gemma. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed pipeline effectively retrieves geospatial information from the spatial database based on natural language queries, as evaluated using Execution Accuracy (EX) and Valid Efficiency Score (VES). © 2026 IEEE.},
