	pages = {317 - 325},
	doi = {10.1145/3800227.3800276},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105038736194?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {China Information Technology Designing & Consulting Institute Co. Ltd, Beijing, China},
	abstract = {With the accelerating digitization of the logistics sector, enterprises have accumulated vast amounts of structured and unstructured data in their daily operations. However, non-technical personnel's lack of proficiency in Structured Query Language (SQL) leads to inefficient data querying, necessitating heavy reliance on specialized IT staff. Natural Language to SQL (NL2SQL) technology offers an effective solution to this challenge. In particular, the rise of Large Language Models (LLMs) presents new opportunities for the semantic parsing of unstructured natural language and the automatic generation of SQL. However, in resource-constrained scenarios, traditional NL2SQL methods struggle to balance accuracy and performance, creating an urgent need for lightweight and adaptable solutions.Focusing on the practical need for logistics policy inquiries, this paper proposes a low-resource NL2SQL system design framework that combines dataset construction, prompt engineering, and Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT). First, a high-quality Chinese NL2SQL dataset containing 986 NL-to-SQL mapping pairs was constructed, covering various types of logistics policies. Second, four types of prompt templates were designed and evaluated to guide the model in generating structurally correct and semantically accurate SQL statements via output field specifications, keyword matching strategies, and temporal expression parsing. Finally, the LoRA method was employed to fine-tune small-parameter models from the DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen series, and performance was evaluated via 5-fold cross-validation. Experimental results show that the fine-tuned 7-billion-parameter model achieved a logical form accuracy of 20.19% using Prompt Template 4, representing a nearly twenty-fold improvement over pre-fine-tuning performance. The fine-tuned 14-billion-parameter model achieved 22.21% logical form accuracy using Prompt Template 4, significantly outperforming the non-fine-tuned model by more than five-fold. This validates the effectiveness of prompt design and PEFT in enhancing generation performance.This study provides a practical paradigm for applying LLMs to NL2SQL tasks in resource-constrained environments. It holds significant importance for enhancing the intelligence of data interaction in the logistics domain and promoting the deployment of large models in vertical industries. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
	author_keywords = {LLM; Logistics domain; NL2SQL; PEFT; Prompt Engineering},
	keywords = {Logistics; Query languages; Query processing; Specifications; Structured Query Language; Systems analysis; Fine tuning; Language model; Large language model; Logistic domain; NL2SQL; Parameter model; Parameter-efficient fine-tuning; Performance; Prompt engineering; Structured Query Language; Tuning},
