	abstract = {This survey provides a comprehensive overview of the transformative impact of Large Language Models (LLMs) on the field of NL2SQL. NL2SQL(aka Text2SQL), the task of converting natural language questions into executable SQL queries, is crucial for democratizing data access, bridging the gap between human language and structured database querying statement. A significant paradigm shift has occurred in this field, redirecting the focus from rule-based method or training models from scratch to adapting pretrained large language models, which has considerably advanced NL2SQL capabilities. The advancements driven by LLMs have not only enhanced accuracy but have also fundamentally altered research methodologies in NL2SQL, transitioning from a focus on model architecture innovation to an emphasis on prompt engineering and data-centric strategies. The core contributions of this survey include a novel taxonomy of LLM-based NL2SQL methods, an in-depth analysis of trends in schema representation, prompt engineering strategies and fine-tuning techniques. Furthermore, this work presents a critical review of benchmark datasets (e.g., Spider, BIRD, SParC) and evaluation methodologies (e.g., Exact Match, Execution Accuracy). Finally, key unresolved challenges, such as handling large schemas, ensuring faithfulness, enabling reasoning over data content, and improving scalability, are identified, alongside promising future research directions poised to shape the next generation of NL2SQL systems. © 2025 IEEE.},
	keywords = {Data accuracy; Engineering research; Natural language processing systems; Query languages; Structured Query Language; Data access; Database querying; Executables; Human language; Language model; Natural language questions; Natural languages; Paradigm shifts; SQL query; Structured database; Query processing},
