	abstract = {Self-Play Fine-Tuning (SPIN) has attracted significant attention in recent years, as it enables large language models (LLMs) to iteratively improve their performance through simulated self-interactions, effectively transforming a weaker model into a stronger one. However, applying SPIN to text-to-SQL tasks remains challenging, primarily due to the model's limited ability to capture implicit schema relationships among database tables during training. To address this limitation, we propose a novel SPIN-based finetuning framework tailored for text-to-SQL tasks, termed EPSP-SQL. Our approach leverages in-context learning to construct explicit prompts that highlight the relationships between database schemas, thereby guiding the model to better understand table interactions during self-play. Experimental results on the Spider dataset demonstrate that EPSP-SQL significantly outperforms existing state-of-the-art methods, achieving superior performance in execution accuracy.  © 2025 IEEE.},
