	affiliations = {MIT Academy of Engineering, Dept. of Computer Engineering, Pune, India},
	abstract = {This work introduces a schema-aware solution that employs the LLaMA 3.2 large language model to translate natural language (NL) queries into executable SQL statements. Unlike traditional text-to-SQL systems that rely on templates or rule-based methods, proposed approach dynamically integrates database schema metadata during inference, enabling more precise and semantically consistent SQL generation. A key novelty of this work lies in the design of a schema-aware agent-emulated pipeline, where SQL generation, validation, and execution are handled by role-specific components emulated through a single LLaMA 3.2 instance. This role separation enhances interpretability, modularity, and safety, while avoiding the need for multiple models or fine-tuning. The system leverages prompt engineering to emulate agent-specific behaviors, ensuring scalability across diverse schema. Real-time extraction and injection of schema metadata from a MySQL database allow accurate mapping of user intents to database structures. The system's effectiveness is demonstrated through multiple evaluation metrics, including BLEU score, syntactic validity, execution accuracy, exact match accuracy, and semantic match score. For real-world applications where data privacy, transparency, and schema sensitivity are critical, proposed method provides a novel, portable, and locally deployable solution. © 2025 IEEE.},
