	abstract = {Text-to-SQL conversion plays a crucial role in streamlining data analysis across various industries. The objective is to generate SQL queries that precisely correspond to natural language prompts and are applicable to an organization's private database, ensuring comprehensive retrieval of necessary information. However, a notable gap in existing research is the lack of comprehensive studies comparing different embeddings and language models for this task. There is a pressing need for structured evaluations to identify optimal models for accurately converting text into SQL queries tailored to private databases commonly used in enterprise settings.To address this gap, we employ the Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) framework. Our study integrates various embedding techniques and large language models (LLMs), utilizing the Retrieval-Augmented Generation Assessment (RAGAs) framework [1] to systematically assess their performance. Evaluation metrics encompass faithfulness, relevancy, context precision, and recall. Our experimental results highlight the effectiveness of our approach in generating accurate SQL queries from natural language inputs.This research makes a significant contribution to advancing the understanding of text-to-SQL conversion methodologies and their real-world applicability in enterprise data environments. © 2025 IEEE.},
