	affiliations = {Ain Shams University, Faculty of Computer & Information Sciences, Artificial Intelligence Department, Cairo, Egypt; Ain Shams University, Faculty of Computer & Information Sciences, Bioinformatics Department, Cairo, Egypt; Ain Shams University, Faculty of Computer & Information Sciences, Basic Sciences Department, Cairo, Egypt},
	abstract = {In today's data-driven world, organizations rely heavily on meaningful insights to make rapid decisions, yet non-technical stakeholders often face challenges when dealing with complex database systems. Traditional reliance on SQL experts or data analysts delays important decision-making. To bridge this gap, we present LexaQuery, a secure, on-premises system that enables users to interact with both SQL and NoSQL (Typesense) databases using plain natural language. LexaQuery is powered by large language models (LLMs), specifically Qwen-Coder 2.5, to provide an easy-to-use chat-based interface that eliminates the need for technical experience and knowledge. The system integrates a multilayer security pipeline ensuring input validation and protecting the model against prompt and SQL injection attacks, achieving 98.28% validation accuracy. For SQL query generation, the Qwen-Coder 2.5 3B model achieves 76.8% accuracy on the Spider benchmark, while the beta version of the NoSQL module, driven by customized prompt engineering, successfully generated valid Typesense queries for 7 out of 10 representative test cases. LexaQuery also includes automated data visualization of the results to enhance the interpretation of results for non-technical users. Experimental results show that the system delivers high accuracy, strong security, and ease of use, significantly reducing the time needed to gain business insights.  © 2025 IEEE.},
